1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Clad in glass, stone or stainless steel, the exterior of a building represents its public face.

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,301
It's more than aesthetics.

3
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:19,000
Years of building regulations, egos, cultural influences, safety concerns and economics

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
have shaped and altered the form of skyscraper architecture.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
The same thing goes for the LA behemoth we're building from the ground up.

6
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,740
Now, Building a Skyscraper, The Exterior, on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:52,860
By the spring of 2003, the builders of the new California Department of Transportation

8
00:00:52,861 --> 00:01:02,740
headquarters have spent 10 months digging, redesigning, shooting, lifting, bolting, and

9
00:01:02,741 --> 00:01:09,800
welding on what will be a 750,000 square foot office building in downtown Los Angeles.

10
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:16,620
The internal steel skeleton has topped out, and now it's time to put a face on the structure,

11
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:18,840
give it an identity.

12
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:25,840
Some believe what a structure looks like is the most important aspect of any large modern

13
00:01:25,841 --> 00:01:26,840
edifice.

14
00:01:26,841 --> 00:01:36,840
The skyscraper is a symbol of economic power, willpower, technological innovation, all bound

15
00:01:36,841 --> 00:01:45,840
up in one highly visible structure that defines the appearance and character of our cities.

16
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:50,810
have always used diverse materials to give classic buildings character.

17
00:01:50,811 --> 00:01:51,720
Terracotta on

18
00:01:51,721 --> 00:02:02,720
the Woolworth Building, stainless steel on the Chrysler Building, or glass on Leverhaus.

19
00:02:02,721 --> 00:02:08,940
Tom Main of Morphosis Architects chose to set the Caltrans Building apart by cladding

20
00:02:08,941 --> 00:02:13,300
it in perforated aluminum panels.

21
00:02:13,301 --> 00:02:16,540
It's not only an aesthetic decision.

22
00:02:16,541 --> 00:02:23,560
The panels will also help conserve energy by shading the structure.

23
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:28,380
But before workers even begin to enclose the building with windows and walls, the general

24
00:02:28,381 --> 00:02:36,780
contractor has decided to construct what's known as a mock-up to test the exterior systems.

25
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:40,220
have become more popular with builders and architects in the last 30 years.

26
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:45,320
We've got a lot of smart people working on this, and they've got some great ideas,

27
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:50,660
but we need to validate the design so that we ensure that it really works,

28
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:53,720
because we only get one shot at it out there on the actual building.

29
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,560
This particular mock-up is a three-story, fully-enclosed structure,

30
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:03,200
complete with window units and an exterior membrane.

31
00:03:03,201 --> 00:03:10,980
Dana Nelson is in charge of subjecting the mock-up to several torturous tests here at

32
00:03:10,981 --> 00:03:16,540
Smith-Emory Laboratories, an independent testing facility located in downtown Los Angeles.

33
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:28,080
We will test for air infiltration, static water penetration, dynamic wind, structural

34
00:03:28,081 --> 00:03:32,160
loading, positive and negative, seismic earthquake testing.

35
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:40,300
The idea is to subject the mock-up to as many extreme situations as possible to make sure

36
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:45,380
the construction materials, like the window units, and the installation techniques, will

37
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:52,360
perform as intended when applied to the Caltrans building.

38
00:03:52,361 --> 00:03:58,120
For three days, Dana throws everything he's got at the mock-up.

39
00:03:58,121 --> 00:04:05,120
This particular piece of equipment is a pre-World War II Pratt Whitney R2800 2,000 horsepower

40
00:04:05,121 --> 00:04:08,440
airplane engine with a 13 and a half foot prop.

41
00:04:08,441 --> 00:04:10,680
What we use this for is dynamic wind testing.

42
00:04:10,681 --> 00:04:14,120
It moves a lot of wind and a lot of water.

43
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:19,460
The fan creates a 63 mile per hour wind.

44
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:25,000
Add some water and that's just this side of a category one hurricane.

45
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:31,000
While the man-made storm rages outside,

46
00:04:31,001 --> 00:04:34,500
inspectors on the inside look for leaks.

47
00:04:34,501 --> 00:04:37,500
If there's water, there's a problem.

48
00:04:37,501 --> 00:04:40,500
Tom, or church?

49
00:04:40,501 --> 00:04:41,500
We got some water here.

50
00:04:41,501 --> 00:04:43,500
We got to find out where it's coming from.

51
00:04:43,501 --> 00:04:47,500
To everyone's disappointment, the mock-up appears to be leaking.

52
00:04:47,501 --> 00:04:48,500
Because it's dry.

53
00:04:48,501 --> 00:04:51,000
The tour now becomes diagnosing the problem.

54
00:04:51,001 --> 00:04:54,000
It is just a puddle, probably two inches in diameter.

55
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:58,580
Is it a materials failure, an installation problem, or a design flaw?

56
00:04:58,581 --> 00:05:00,240
It has to be wet on the left side.

57
00:05:00,241 --> 00:05:00,941
Right.

58
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:01,760
And it's not.

59
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:03,460
This whole south end was fogged up.

60
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,800
So all the mow ends were a little wet.

61
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:10,440
Will the leaking issue be a major setback or require only a minor adjustment?

62
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:13,000
It's not immediately clear.

63
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:15,500
They left all to be dry.

64
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:20,720
Mock-up testing like this can help to prevent potential problems with the exterior cladding.

65
00:05:20,721 --> 00:05:27,720
But testing the structural system of a building on a large scale is much more difficult.

66
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:34,720
A small mock-up doesn't necessarily react the same way a full-sized building will.

67
00:05:34,721 --> 00:05:41,720
There are even a few rare examples of buildings going up before a structural problem is even diagnosed.

68
00:05:41,721 --> 00:05:48,720
One of the most extreme examples is that of the Citicorp Center in New York City,

69
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:53,080
where builders had to deal with the potential collapse of their finished structure.

70
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:03,820
In 1978, the newly opened Citicorp Center seemed to hover 10 stories in the air on four legs.

71
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,660
But unlike a table, the building supports were placed in the center of each wall.

72
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:17,100
The building owed part of its radical design to St. Peter's Lutheran Church,

73
00:06:17,101 --> 00:06:20,300
which had occupied the same corner since 1904.

74
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:25,480
Developers wanted to buy out the church

75
00:06:25,481 --> 00:06:28,460
so they could have the entire lot to construct their building.

76
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,980
But the congregation didn't want to give up their house of worship.

77
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,780
Renowned structural engineer William LeMessure

78
00:06:36,781 --> 00:06:39,520
came up with a way of satisfying both parties.

79
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:43,380
Finally, I said the solution to this problem

80
00:06:43,381 --> 00:06:46,180
is to put the column in the middle of each side

81
00:06:46,181 --> 00:06:50,780
and then the building can cantilever, hang over the church.

82
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:55,160
Citicorp offered to tear down the decaying St. Peter's

83
00:06:55,161 --> 00:06:56,280
and build a new church,

84
00:06:56,860 --> 00:07:00,320
above which they would construct their 59-story skyscraper.

85
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,120
The building performed its magic trick

86
00:07:06,121 --> 00:07:07,980
with the help of giant V-braces

87
00:07:07,981 --> 00:07:10,920
that transferred the gravity and wind loads

88
00:07:10,921 --> 00:07:12,040
to the center columns.

89
00:07:12,041 --> 00:07:20,580
The building was completed in 1977 and opened to much fanfare.

90
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:30,500
But just months later, the structural engineer on Citicorp got a call from an inquisitive engineering student.

91
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:36,160
He wanted to know why didn't we put the Collins in the corners like a sensible person would do.

92
00:07:36,161 --> 00:07:39,680
And I explained that we developed this triangulation system

93
00:07:39,681 --> 00:07:43,400
to bring all the loads to the middle of each side instead of the corners.

94
00:07:44,780 --> 00:07:48,800
The V-braces also helped the building withstand perpendicular winds,

95
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:50,760
winds hitting the building head-on.

96
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:54,540
But the phone call got him thinking.

97
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:57,660
Shortly before the student's innocent call,

98
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:01,700
LeMessur learned of a design change that now concerned him greatly.

99
00:08:01,701 --> 00:08:14,059
To save time and money during construction, the steel subcontractor had suggested using bolted connections on the V-bracing, instead of the welded connections

100
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:15,780
originally called for.

101
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:21,820
Changes like this happen all the time on big projects.

102
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:28,120
The modification met building code, and LeMessur's office and other parties signed off on the alteration.

103
00:08:28,121 --> 00:08:36,480
But the bolted connections LeMessur now realized were especially susceptible to quartering winds,

104
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:42,880
winds that hit a building from a 45-degree angle, putting greater stress on two sides.

105
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:48,700
So I made some calculations, and I found out how strong these bolts were,

106
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:52,580
and I said, oh, Mike, we are in big trouble,

107
00:08:52,581 --> 00:08:58,060
because a wind in a typhoon and hurricanes and so forth

108
00:08:58,061 --> 00:09:02,600
would develop forces that would be far beyond the capacity of these bolts

109
00:09:02,601 --> 00:09:05,320
and that the building could fail.

110
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:09,800
Wind tunnel testing confirmed his worst fears.

111
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:15,120
A sizable windstorm, the kind that hits New York about once every 16 years,

112
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:16,680
could topple the building.

113
00:09:16,681 --> 00:09:24,500
William LeMessure, a renowned structural engineer, now faced a dilemma.

114
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:28,320
Should he speak up and risk his outstanding reputation,

115
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:32,840
or shut up and potentially risk the lives of the building's occupants?

116
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:39,060
Here I have this knowledge. I have to do something about it.

117
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,120
He alerted the owner of the building.

118
00:09:42,121 --> 00:09:50,460
LeMessur and an assembled team devised a way of reinforcing the susceptible connections

119
00:09:50,461 --> 00:09:53,740
by welding two-inch thick steel plates over them.

120
00:09:55,740 --> 00:10:00,320
Welders worked feverishly under the cover of night to avoid alarming the building's occupants.

121
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:08,080
Then on September 1st, 1978, LeMessur got the weather report he was fearing.

122
00:10:08,081 --> 00:10:13,080
Hurricane Ella was headed straight for New York City.

123
00:10:13,081 --> 00:10:17,080
I was scared to death because we weren't quite ready yet, you see.

124
00:10:17,081 --> 00:10:21,080
We hadn't completed all the welding that needed to be done.

125
00:10:21,081 --> 00:10:27,080
Fortunately, the storm changed course, and the building averted possible disaster.

126
00:10:27,081 --> 00:10:32,080
Within a few weeks, welders completed retrofitting the building.

127
00:10:32,081 --> 00:10:37,080
And today, the structure is stronger than ever, as is William LeMessur's reputation.

128
00:10:37,081 --> 00:10:42,080
The Citicorp incident has only added to his fame.

129
00:10:42,081 --> 00:10:49,480
It's hard to know what difference a mock-up might have made for the Citicorp Center.

130
00:10:49,481 --> 00:10:54,880
But in Los Angeles, the Caltrans mock-up testing is at least preventing more minor exterior

131
00:10:54,881 --> 00:10:55,880
issues.

132
00:10:55,881 --> 00:10:57,980
Okay, it's not worth a slice of that.

133
00:10:57,981 --> 00:10:59,180
That could be...

134
00:10:59,181 --> 00:11:02,180
But after two days of testing, there are still questions.

135
00:11:02,181 --> 00:11:05,180
Up, in, up, in, up, one inch and a goal.

136
00:11:05,181 --> 00:11:06,180
That's impossible.

137
00:11:06,181 --> 00:11:08,620
It was cold in my handle and it was completely dry.

138
00:11:08,621 --> 00:11:10,860
I don't see no rhyme or reason why it's there.

139
00:11:10,861 --> 00:11:15,060
The static water test earlier we had a couple problems with.

140
00:11:15,061 --> 00:11:19,800
We think that we've isolated them and we made some repairs and as we go through we'll repeat

141
00:11:19,801 --> 00:11:24,220
it until we get a clean test on every element.

142
00:11:24,221 --> 00:11:28,580
It will take further testing to find out if this little leak will turn into a financial

143
00:11:28,581 --> 00:11:30,800
drain for the general contractor.

144
00:11:30,801 --> 00:11:33,820
They've got to work fast.

145
00:11:33,821 --> 00:11:37,220
on the wall system begins in less than two months.

146
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:43,280
From the decorative buttresses of the Neo-Gothic Tribune Tower in Chicago,

147
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:48,040
to the sleek lines of the Art Deco RCA building in New York,

148
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:52,020
to the glass facade of the John Hancock Tower in Boston,

149
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:57,340
architects of tall structures have struggled to create exteriors

150
00:11:57,341 --> 00:12:00,140
that were as memorable as the buildings were massive.

151
00:12:00,141 --> 00:12:05,140
LA's Caltrans building is no exception.

152
00:12:05,141 --> 00:12:07,140
We're putting it in 1st and Main.

153
00:12:07,141 --> 00:12:10,140
It's literally the center of the city, because it's a great site, right?

154
00:12:10,141 --> 00:12:13,140
It's a conceptual, historic center of the city, next to City Hall.

155
00:12:13,141 --> 00:12:20,140
An architect has to weigh practical, financial, and aesthetic considerations during design.

156
00:12:20,141 --> 00:12:29,140
Struggling against often opposing objectives, the architect sometimes, miraculously, creates art.

157
00:12:29,141 --> 00:12:38,900
Skyscraper architects today have over 120 years of successful and not so successful

158
00:12:38,901 --> 00:12:44,180
creations to look back upon and learn from.

159
00:12:44,181 --> 00:12:49,640
Since the first skyscraper was built in the 1880s, distinct styles emerged as a reflection

160
00:12:49,641 --> 00:12:52,420
of the times and the technology available.

161
00:12:52,421 --> 00:12:59,420
But they were shaped by building regulations and egos as well.

162
00:12:59,421 --> 00:13:04,420
Many scholars consider Chicago to be the birthplace of skyscrapers

163
00:13:04,421 --> 00:13:09,420
and the designer Louis Sullivan to be the father of skyscraper architecture.

164
00:13:09,421 --> 00:13:14,420
The most famous name for the beginnings of the skyscrapers, Louis Sullivan,

165
00:13:14,421 --> 00:13:19,420
a Chicago architect who came up with a formula for the tall building

166
00:13:19,421 --> 00:13:24,420
for the tall building that accentuated its verticality.

167
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:28,020
He said that the skyscraper, or the tall office building,

168
00:13:28,060 --> 00:13:31,060
as he called it, should be tall, every inch of it tall.

169
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:35,060
It should be a proud and soaring thing.

170
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:36,700
Sullivan expressed these opinions

171
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:39,380
in his Wainwright building.

172
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:42,180
Louis Sullivan had the idea to treat a building

173
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:46,940
like a classical column with a base and a tall shaft

174
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:48,220
and a capital.

175
00:13:48,221 --> 00:13:55,180
So the early skyscrapers have a base of three or four stories and then a very tall shaft

176
00:13:55,181 --> 00:14:04,780
and then usually some sort of a capital, which would be a cornice or a slightly setback top floor or a big ornate roof.

177
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:09,620
But that's as close to classical form as Sullivan came.

178
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:16,740
He and other Chicago architects like Daniel Burnham searched for a new and uniquely American style.

179
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:21,820
It came to be known as the Chicago School.

180
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:26,500
The first Chicago School of the 1880s and 90s

181
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:31,800
was represented with a kind of aesthetic theory of simplicity

182
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:35,640
that made the buildings look simple in comparison

183
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:39,900
to their counterparts in New York, where New York architects

184
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,480
were much more wedded to historical style.

185
00:14:43,580 --> 00:14:46,140
One thinks of Cass Gilbert and the Woolworth building

186
00:14:46,141 --> 00:14:47,980
called the Cathedral of Commerce.

187
00:14:49,020 --> 00:14:50,720
It's a neo-Gothic building.

188
00:14:51,720 --> 00:14:54,420
That language of historical styles

189
00:14:54,421 --> 00:14:56,280
was much more popular in New York,

190
00:14:56,420 --> 00:15:01,260
whereas the Chicago School was called in its time

191
00:15:01,261 --> 00:15:05,060
and by subsequent scholars a real American architecture.

192
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,600
Upon its completion in 1913,

193
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:11,560
New York's Woolworth Building

194
00:15:11,561 --> 00:15:16,060
became the tallest skyscraper in the world at 792 feet.

195
00:15:16,140 --> 00:15:22,560
By the early 1900s, the streets of New York were becoming urban canyons,

196
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:25,800
planked by towering spans of glass and stone.

197
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:33,060
Seeing the negative effects of overbuilding, people started calling for construction regulation.

198
00:15:35,220 --> 00:15:40,660
The new equitable building, completed in 1915, was one of the last straws.

199
00:15:40,661 --> 00:15:49,100
The massive structure rose 40 stories, straight up, like a cliff, casting a seven-acre shadow

200
00:15:49,101 --> 00:15:53,400
across the city.

201
00:15:53,401 --> 00:15:58,660
Fearing other buildings like it would suffocate the streets below and decrease property values,

202
00:15:58,661 --> 00:16:06,140
New York passed a groundbreaking zoning ordinance in 1916 to limit the mass of buildings.

203
00:16:06,141 --> 00:16:09,680
The 1916 zoning law in New York was tremendously important

204
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:13,460
for the formal development of the New York skyscraper

205
00:16:13,461 --> 00:16:17,240
and gave us the characteristic form of the setback tower,

206
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:20,660
a kind of stepped pyramid, a ziggurat,

207
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:25,980
or a setback form that some people call the wedding cake.

208
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:29,760
Under the new zoning ordinance,

209
00:16:29,761 --> 00:16:31,960
a building could only rise so high

210
00:16:31,961 --> 00:16:33,760
before stepping back from the street.

211
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:38,020
The higher the building, the more setbacks it required.

212
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:43,680
However, setback laws didn't restrict building height.

213
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:48,680
A tower that occupied no more than 25% of the lot

214
00:16:48,681 --> 00:16:52,740
was allowed to rise as high as technology could push it.

215
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:57,760
When you think of the classic 1920s towers

216
00:16:57,761 --> 00:17:01,600
like the Chrysler Building or the Empire State,

217
00:17:01,601 --> 00:17:06,720
those slender spires rising from a kind of pyramidal base

218
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:12,040
are the characteristic Art Deco skyscraper of the 1920s.

219
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:16,520
And their form is really dictated by a template,

220
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:21,100
a three-dimensional template that's decreed by the 1916 zoning law.

221
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:25,660
Sure, the New York towers of the 1920s and early 30s were tall,

222
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:30,240
but they were also extravagant, reflecting the exuberance of the Jazz Age.

223
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:38,600
Perhaps no building captured the excitement of the era better than the Chrysler Building.

224
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:41,478
Built by automobile magnate Walter P.

225
00:17:41,479 --> 00:17:45,600
Chrysler and designed by architect William Van Allen,

226
00:17:45,601 --> 00:17:50,600
the Chrysler Building borrowed its motif from its namesake's product.

227
00:17:50,601 --> 00:17:57,600
The building came complete with hood ornament gargoyles and hubcaps.

228
00:17:57,601 --> 00:17:58,304
pubcaps.

229
00:17:58,305 --> 00:18:05,340
Although a favorite among New Yorkers today, the building was criticized by many

230
00:18:05,341 --> 00:18:08,840
as being too flamboyant when completed in 1930.

231
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:13,786
And it becomes so large that it's just kind of a sculptural device.

232
00:18:13,787 --> 00:18:14,620
And these are all

233
00:18:14,621 --> 00:18:16,474
lit from below and they just glow.

234
00:18:16,475 --> 00:18:19,200
And it's literally, they're these concrete pieces.

235
00:18:19,220 --> 00:18:23,700
Although the architects of the Caltrans project won't be pushing the envelope as far as William

236
00:18:23,701 --> 00:18:28,060
Van Allen did with the Chrysler Building, they're still confronted with the same challenge,

237
00:18:28,620 --> 00:18:33,800
how to create a memorable and satisfying public face for a very large structure.

238
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:41,240
It's also complicated. Something you hate today, you actually admire 10 years later.

239
00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:46,520
And we can't worry about working in reverse, let's do a building that nobody doesn't like.

240
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:50,020
It's totally stupid, because now we produce everything that's just in a neutral,

241
00:18:50,120 --> 00:18:51,620
we're going to all go to sleep as a society.

242
00:18:51,621 --> 00:18:54,380
You have a whole series of buildings now that nobody cares about.

243
00:18:54,381 --> 00:18:58,180
But when you see the skin and you look at the real one and you realize it has holes in it

244
00:18:58,181 --> 00:19:01,800
and it builds up colors and the thing behind and this and then you layer light on it,

245
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:04,680
it could be, it could work.

246
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:12,100
Tom has high hopes for the extreme-looking cladding of perforated aluminum panels he's chosen for this building.

247
00:19:14,600 --> 00:19:18,320
As Tom and his team struggle with the last remaining design elements,

248
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:24,940
Clark Construction is trying to figure out how to get 306,000 square feet of glass produced,

249
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:30,880
windows assembled, and units transported from the heart of Texas to Los Angeles.

250
00:19:31,960 --> 00:19:34,320
They've got three months to make it happen.

251
00:19:38,180 --> 00:19:42,860
After 10 months of construction on our Caltrans building, features are emerging.

252
00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:47,700
When the steel is erected, you can start reading the shape of the building.

253
00:19:47,701 --> 00:19:50,940
It's not enclosed here, but you can see the major monument spaces.

254
00:19:51,460 --> 00:19:52,880
Here, there is the dining area.

255
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:53,700
This is the cafe.

256
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:57,980
Just in front of the main entrance of the building,

257
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:01,060
there is a huge light well,

258
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:03,700
which is bringing daylight through the building

259
00:20:03,701 --> 00:20:05,700
all the way down to the urban lobby.

260
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:08,800
So if you look up, you're going to see the skylight.

261
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:12,140
Once the structural steel is up,

262
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:15,980
the next step is to enclose the building with walls and windows.

263
00:20:17,700 --> 00:20:31,700
Kramers start building the walls by attaching metal studs to the steel beams to create the

264
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:34,520
base for the wall system.

265
00:20:34,521 --> 00:20:40,300
Right now, my partner and I, we're going to start setting up the stud for the exterior

266
00:20:40,301 --> 00:20:41,300
wall.

267
00:20:41,301 --> 00:20:47,540
We have a laser with a tripod that's going to establish the level line.

268
00:20:47,541 --> 00:20:54,020
looking for 11 and a quarter I have 12 and a half go up partner just right there 11 and a quarter

269
00:20:54,021 --> 00:21:02,840
money I like it plump it the exterior wall system is a combination of multi layers it starts from

270
00:21:02,841 --> 00:21:08,240
the metal stud framing this sheathing board is provides another layer in which the single ply

271
00:21:08,241 --> 00:21:15,380
roofing membrane is applied this is ultimately the base waterproofing of the wall system on this

272
00:21:15,381 --> 00:21:18,980
project, Morphosis architects are using a material called

273
00:21:18,981 --> 00:21:20,920
sarnophyll.

274
00:21:20,921 --> 00:21:26,140
It's a thermoplastic membrane, usually used for roofing.

275
00:21:26,141 --> 00:21:30,180
But Morphosis is applying it on vertical surfaces.

276
00:21:30,181 --> 00:21:31,880
It's like wrapping the building in cellophane.

277
00:21:34,620 --> 00:21:37,980
They apply the glue, lay the matting over the top of that,

278
00:21:37,981 --> 00:21:42,020
and then work out the bubbles.

279
00:21:42,021 --> 00:21:44,880
These walls are known as curtain walls,

280
00:21:44,881 --> 00:21:48,200
because they hang from the building and don't support any real weight.

281
00:21:50,420 --> 00:21:55,520
Nearly every large building constructed since 1900 has used curtain wall construction.

282
00:21:57,860 --> 00:22:02,160
Made possible by the introduction of the internal steel cage in the 1880s,

283
00:22:02,161 --> 00:22:05,860
the curtain wall gave architects even more freedom of expression.

284
00:22:09,920 --> 00:22:13,840
Because they weren't load-bearing, the curtain walls could be light,

285
00:22:13,841 --> 00:22:18,840
thinner at the base, and provide larger window openings.

286
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:22,840
And having larger windows was an enormous benefit

287
00:22:22,841 --> 00:22:24,840
in an era before air conditioning

288
00:22:24,841 --> 00:22:26,840
and efficient electric lighting.

289
00:22:26,841 --> 00:22:30,840
One never had a floor space in a first-class office building

290
00:22:30,841 --> 00:22:33,840
that was farther than 28 feet from the outside window

291
00:22:33,841 --> 00:22:35,840
to the interior corridor.

292
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:42,840
Chicago's Reliance Building, completed in 1895,

293
00:22:42,841 --> 00:22:45,860
was a fine example of early curtain wall construction

294
00:22:45,861 --> 00:22:48,540
and a predictor of things to come.

295
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:53,480
The building's massive windows attracted doctors and dentists,

296
00:22:53,920 --> 00:22:55,480
whose work depended on good light.

297
00:22:56,400 --> 00:23:00,380
Actually, they had problems filling the building originally when it first opened.

298
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:03,840
They'd never seen such construction with so much glass on the exterior.

299
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:08,720
And Chicagoans thought the building was actually going to blow over in the windy city.

300
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:12,500
Fortunately, the Reliance building never blew over.

301
00:23:12,840 --> 00:23:20,780
And after extensive renovation, the Hotel Burnham now invites guests to peer out those same large windows at the city below.

302
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:31,800
Windows are just as important in the construction of today's buildings.

303
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:38,520
These are the strip window units we're installing out here at the Caltrans District 7 headquarters.

304
00:23:39,320 --> 00:23:42,440
We have around 1,000 of these to put in.

305
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:46,980
The weight of that unit's figured right around 300 pounds.

306
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:49,200
So we got four guys on it.

307
00:23:49,240 --> 00:23:52,360
Every guy's lifting a reasonable amount.

308
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:56,140
These units are surprisingly sophisticated,

309
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:00,740
double-paned, and coated with an energy-conserving film

310
00:24:00,741 --> 00:24:02,460
that reduces heat transmittance.

311
00:24:04,800 --> 00:24:08,200
But it takes many players to create this space-age product.

312
00:24:08,201 --> 00:24:18,020
The window units begin their 1,500-mile odyssey in Wichita Falls, Texas, where PPG Industries,

313
00:24:18,560 --> 00:24:22,280
the nation's leading glassmaker, produces the raw glass.

314
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:29,040
The process of glass starts out with the raw materials area where we load bins with the

315
00:24:29,041 --> 00:24:29,840
raw materials.

316
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:32,580
The main ingredients are soda ash and sand.

317
00:24:32,860 --> 00:24:38,120
We mix all the materials together and we basically pour them into a big furnace, which we call

318
00:24:38,121 --> 00:24:38,821
the tank.

319
00:24:38,822 --> 00:24:45,120
The temperature varies between 2600 and 2900 degrees to melt all the raw materials.

320
00:24:46,120 --> 00:24:52,120
At that point it pours into what we call the flow bath, where the molten glass actually

321
00:24:52,121 --> 00:24:54,373
floats on top of liquid tin.

322
00:24:54,374 --> 00:24:59,120
The temperature moves from about 2000 degrees down to about

323
00:24:59,121 --> 00:25:05,120
1100 degrees at what we call the lift out roll that goes up onto what we call the annealing

324
00:25:05,121 --> 00:25:11,761
layer. The annealing layer cools the glass down.

325
00:25:11,762 --> 00:25:16,880
And then the glass is cut to size and

326
00:25:16,881 --> 00:25:21,945
the cutting area we call the wear room.

327
00:25:21,946 --> 00:25:27,920
Some of the plants can be a quarter mile long.

328
00:25:27,921 --> 00:25:41,921
Once the glass is cut, PPG applies a low emissivity, or low E, energy conserving coating.

329
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:52,240
The coating lets visible light in, but blocks 90% of infrared heat, thus reducing heat transmittance.

330
00:25:52,241 --> 00:25:55,580
Accurate Systems of Sunnyvale, Texas, is responsible for

331
00:25:55,581 --> 00:25:58,840
assembling the window units.

332
00:25:58,841 --> 00:26:03,120
We fabricate the pieces, cut them to length, do all the

333
00:26:03,121 --> 00:26:04,120
fabrication.

334
00:26:04,121 --> 00:26:10,720
A job like the Carl Tran job has over two million pieces that

335
00:26:10,721 --> 00:26:11,720
we have to produce.

336
00:26:38,306 --> 00:26:42,360
After a three-day drive, the semi pulls into the Caltrans site.

337
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:46,680
Another truckload down, 72 truckloads to go.

338
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:50,920
A building without windows in it sounds like jail.

339
00:26:50,921 --> 00:26:55,080
There's kind of a sense of freedom to be able to look out.

340
00:26:56,240 --> 00:26:58,940
Even though you might have to be at your job all day long,

341
00:26:59,440 --> 00:27:02,640
you do have some sort of a connection with the outside world.

342
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:06,680
That connection with the outside world got an enormous boost

343
00:27:06,681 --> 00:27:08,580
when in the mid-20th century,

344
00:27:09,260 --> 00:27:12,920
architects started cladding buildings from top to bottom in glass.

345
00:27:15,860 --> 00:27:17,660
It was the face of the future,

346
00:27:17,661 --> 00:27:23,420
and it would dramatically change the look of the modern skyscraper.

347
00:27:25,980 --> 00:27:33,400
Five months after topping out, the guys with model glass are 85% finished with the installation of window units.

348
00:27:35,700 --> 00:27:41,860
The glaziers have moved to the south side of the building, which has much larger floor-to-ceiling window units.

349
00:27:43,740 --> 00:27:46,780
We're going to set all these outside windows on this bottom track.

350
00:27:47,660 --> 00:27:53,400
But they're about 500 pounds, so we've got to use a cup manipulator,

351
00:27:53,401 --> 00:27:56,100
and that just takes the weight because they're too heavy to set by hand.

352
00:27:57,040 --> 00:27:58,700
Hold on, we're hooked on the side there.

353
00:28:02,200 --> 00:28:06,000
The love affair between skyscrapers and glass began in earnest

354
00:28:06,001 --> 00:28:08,820
with the economic explosion of the 1950s.

355
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:16,020
But one building was decades ahead of the trend.

356
00:28:16,021 --> 00:28:21,220
the Philadelphia Savings Fund Society Building, completed in 1932.

357
00:28:22,300 --> 00:28:26,540
The PSFS building was the first skyscraper cast in the international style,

358
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:30,400
the language of developing modernism in the 20th century.

359
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:35,840
The PSFS building was designed with sealed windows

360
00:28:35,841 --> 00:28:40,240
and no ornamentation beyond the 27-foot-tall bank letters on the roof.

361
00:28:40,241 --> 00:28:48,880
Influenced by European functionalist design of the 1920s, it was a dramatic departure

362
00:28:48,881 --> 00:28:56,160
from contemporary skyscrapers, such as the Empire State or Chrysler buildings.

363
00:28:56,161 --> 00:29:02,020
American architect George Howe and Swiss foreign partner William Lesquise created a building

364
00:29:02,021 --> 00:29:07,420
to project the bank's image of luxury, functionality, and modernity.

365
00:29:07,421 --> 00:29:14,420
PSFS had the qualities that predicted the direction that modern architecture would go

366
00:29:14,421 --> 00:29:16,420
after World War II.

367
00:29:16,421 --> 00:29:23,420
In the 1950s, after years of depression and war, an economic boom sent skyscraper

368
00:29:23,421 --> 00:29:30,420
construction soaring once again, this time with a fresh modern look.

369
00:29:30,421 --> 00:29:36,980
Dubbed the international style, architects embraced steel and glass, minimalism and function.

370
00:29:37,740 --> 00:29:45,420
There was a new kind of building, a welded steel frame, a glass facade that was hermetically sealed in effect.

371
00:29:45,520 --> 00:29:52,640
No longer windows that opened, but a well-tempered environment with air conditioning, mechanical systems, fluorescent lights.

372
00:29:53,420 --> 00:30:00,200
In 1952, the Lever Brothers Soap Company completed Lever House on Park Avenue in New York City.

373
00:30:00,420 --> 00:30:07,420
The cool green tinted glass and stainless steel exterior projected an image of cleanliness

374
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:11,311
and purity, just right for a soap company.

375
00:30:11,312 --> 00:30:15,080
Lieberhaus was one of the first all glass curtain

376
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:21,449
skyscrapers and would come to epitomize the international style.

377
00:30:21,450 --> 00:30:23,300
Across the street a few

378
00:30:23,301 --> 00:30:25,480
Across the street a few years later,

379
00:30:26,100 --> 00:30:29,960
Philip Johnson and German-born architect Ludwig Mies van der Rohe

380
00:30:29,961 --> 00:30:31,880
collaborated on the Seagram Building,

381
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:36,360
an austere, elegantly simple black box of a skyscraper.

382
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:41,680
Mies van der Rohe was an enormous influence

383
00:30:41,681 --> 00:30:44,000
on post-war American architecture generally

384
00:30:44,001 --> 00:30:45,920
and certainly on the skyscraper.

385
00:30:45,921 --> 00:30:48,140
His maxim, less is more,

386
00:30:48,620 --> 00:30:51,920
became an influence on so many architects.

387
00:30:53,300 --> 00:30:59,720
Through the 1960s and 70s, developers built hundreds of glass-encapsulated skyscrapers

388
00:30:59,721 --> 00:31:01,720
with minimal ornamentation.

389
00:31:01,721 --> 00:31:10,120
Architects purposefully avoided all symbolic qualities on their buildings.

390
00:31:10,121 --> 00:31:16,700
The most famous buildings of that period, like the Seagrass building, are all flat-topped,

391
00:31:16,701 --> 00:31:21,700
and they are rectangular, abstract boxes,

392
00:31:21,701 --> 00:31:24,700
which I understand, I understand the roots of this.

393
00:31:24,701 --> 00:31:27,700
I happen to believe that it was a mistaken notion

394
00:31:27,701 --> 00:31:29,700
applied to tall buildings.

395
00:31:29,701 --> 00:31:32,700
I believe that you cannot avoid the symbolic qualities

396
00:31:32,701 --> 00:31:35,700
of a building when it reaches the sky.

397
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:43,700
Finally, by the mid-1970s,

398
00:31:43,701 --> 00:31:46,700
architects were once again ready for something new.

399
00:31:46,701 --> 00:31:53,700
Late in the 20th century, architects start to think beyond the stripped-down, modernist

400
00:31:53,701 --> 00:31:54,700
box.

401
00:31:54,701 --> 00:31:59,700
Philip Johnson designs the AT&T building in New York City with a top that makes it look

402
00:31:59,701 --> 00:32:02,700
like a Chippendale dresser.

403
00:32:02,701 --> 00:32:09,700
After years of embracing the minimalist style, Johnson found himself rebelling against the

404
00:32:09,701 --> 00:32:12,700
form he helped create.

405
00:32:12,701 --> 00:32:19,480
The head of AT&T said, I want to be different from all this boxy stuff like the Sea Room building.

406
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:21,460
I said, that's right, let's be different.

407
00:32:22,580 --> 00:32:27,740
I went to classical, I went to Hadrian, I went to early Christian churches.

408
00:32:28,480 --> 00:32:33,520
Actually, I got that famous cut, not from Chippendale, but where Chippendale got it from,

409
00:32:34,060 --> 00:32:37,640
which was the pediments of ancient Roman Greeks.

410
00:32:37,641 --> 00:32:44,300
In the last 20 years, builders have erected elegant towers and shimmering spires.

411
00:32:46,360 --> 00:32:49,480
Some look more like modern art than skyscrapers.

412
00:32:51,500 --> 00:32:54,740
Not since the days of the Woolworth Building and the Chrysler Building

413
00:32:54,741 --> 00:32:57,740
have skyscrapers been as free to express themselves.

414
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:06,660
Although born in America, skyscrapers have spread to dense urban centers around the world.

415
00:33:07,640 --> 00:33:16,640
But today, developers also have to be sensitive when importing an icon of Western capitalism

416
00:33:16,641 --> 00:33:21,280
into a society steeped in tradition.

417
00:33:21,281 --> 00:33:26,660
The architects of the Taipei 101 in Taiwan labored to make their building represent more

418
00:33:26,661 --> 00:33:31,160
than corporate identity and financial prosperity.

419
00:33:31,161 --> 00:33:36,520
When we start looking at this building, we like to think it's growing from the earth.

420
00:33:36,521 --> 00:33:42,180
like the bamboo in our culture, which is supple.

421
00:33:42,181 --> 00:33:45,400
It bends in the wind, but not breaking.

422
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:49,900
The bamboo-inspired tower has eight nodes.

423
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:56,720
We use the lucky number eight as a basis for the pot,

424
00:33:56,980 --> 00:33:59,660
and we put eight pots together on top of each other,

425
00:34:00,120 --> 00:34:02,000
create this basic form.

426
00:34:02,900 --> 00:34:05,900
The exterior is adorned with symbols of abundance

427
00:34:05,901 --> 00:34:08,900
and prosperity.

428
00:34:08,901 --> 00:34:12,900
We added the symbols from our culture,

429
00:34:12,901 --> 00:34:22,900
such as Ruyi, and the coin, the ancient coins,

430
00:34:22,901 --> 00:34:26,900
all to make it with very strong characters

431
00:34:26,901 --> 00:34:29,900
facing our culture.

432
00:34:29,901 --> 00:34:34,900
I think the design is the soul of a building.

433
00:34:34,901 --> 00:34:36,820
To me, architecture is like film.

434
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:39,160
If it's a really good film, not only is it coherent,

435
00:34:39,520 --> 00:34:43,640
but it finally really somehow moves you in that coherency and it says something.

436
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:45,120
It speaks to you somehow.

437
00:34:45,820 --> 00:34:46,620
Ditto architecture.

438
00:34:48,440 --> 00:34:53,020
But today, the design of a building must be more than an aesthetic consideration.

439
00:34:54,120 --> 00:34:56,860
Increasing energy costs and dwindling resources

440
00:34:56,861 --> 00:35:00,200
necessitate more environmentally conscious construction.

441
00:35:01,740 --> 00:35:04,220
The latest trend in skyscraper architecture?

442
00:35:04,900 --> 00:35:11,900
sustainability. More and more buildings are going green.

443
00:35:11,901 --> 00:35:18,900
In the last century, technology allowed buildings to grow to mammoth proportions.

444
00:35:18,901 --> 00:35:24,900
Architects sealed up structures, thanks to the introduction of mechanical heating and cooling.

445
00:35:24,901 --> 00:35:32,900
In the process, they tossed aside alternative forms of climate control, such as awnings for shading

446
00:35:32,901 --> 00:35:36,780
and operable windows that help define building exteriors.

447
00:35:38,640 --> 00:35:41,920
But comfort and architectural freedom came with a price.

448
00:35:43,500 --> 00:35:45,800
Big buildings have voracious appetites.

449
00:35:46,960 --> 00:35:50,100
I think one of the most dramatic things that we have to realize is

450
00:35:50,101 --> 00:35:55,840
buildings account for more than a third of all energy used today in the United States.

451
00:35:56,340 --> 00:35:59,600
We know that cities must be built with density.

452
00:35:59,601 --> 00:36:06,280
The question now is how do we make tall buildings that are sustainable, that are environmentally friendly?

453
00:36:06,580 --> 00:36:13,920
And that marks the quest for architects and designers in the construction of 21st century skyscrapers.

454
00:36:14,820 --> 00:36:22,240
The trend began more than 25 years ago when conscientious designers, engineers, manufacturers and builders

455
00:36:22,241 --> 00:36:26,040
started working together on a different kind of green revolution.

456
00:36:26,041 --> 00:36:33,040
The goal? Build more sustainable, or green, skyscrapers.

457
00:36:33,041 --> 00:36:34,551
In 2000, the U.S.

458
00:36:34,552 --> 00:36:41,040
Green Building Council established the LEED Green Building Rating System,

459
00:36:41,041 --> 00:36:46,040
a benchmark by which a structure's sustainability can be measured.

460
00:36:46,041 --> 00:36:52,040
The LEED Building Rating System, which is an acronym for Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design,

461
00:36:52,041 --> 00:36:57,500
was essentially developed to help transform the marketplace towards green buildings.

462
00:36:58,860 --> 00:37:02,740
Studies show an investment of less than 2% of the price of the building

463
00:37:02,741 --> 00:37:07,900
can produce a structure that uses 30% less energy and 50% less water.

464
00:37:09,960 --> 00:37:14,520
Conservation was a principal objective for the state of California on the Caltrans project.

465
00:37:15,700 --> 00:37:19,780
The structure will be one of the largest, greenest buildings on the West Coast.

466
00:37:22,040 --> 00:37:27,140
Accomplishing that task means that the building's exterior will be completed in a most unique fashion.

467
00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:35,500
The focus generally in sustainable buildings is to, in fact, develop a high-performance facade,

468
00:37:35,501 --> 00:37:39,120
or a facade that optimizes heat gain and heat loss.

469
00:37:39,980 --> 00:37:44,420
An enormous amount of interior heat or cold escapes through the skin of a building.

470
00:37:46,740 --> 00:37:50,680
In addition, direct sunlight creates unwanted heat in the summer.

471
00:37:52,040 --> 00:38:00,020
To minimize the solar gain on the Caltrans building, Morphosis architects are using 181,000

472
00:38:00,021 --> 00:38:06,360
square feet of perforated aluminum cladding called scrim as a shading mechanism.

473
00:38:06,361 --> 00:38:11,360
Here we come, Al.

474
00:38:11,361 --> 00:38:12,360
Ready?

475
00:38:12,361 --> 00:38:21,820
What I'm doing here is checking the scrim panel to see if it lines up with the latching

476
00:38:21,821 --> 00:38:31,860
arm and what we're doing here today is installing this pneumatic actuator and it will control

477
00:38:31,861 --> 00:38:38,820
the opening and closing of the scrim panels and that's all controlled again through a

478
00:38:38,821 --> 00:38:39,820
computer.

479
00:38:39,821 --> 00:38:46,260
Kurt, can you go ahead and close the scrim panels?

480
00:38:46,261 --> 00:38:50,440
We have 1,031 actuators throughout the whole job.

481
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:54,580
Okay, all the scrim panels look like they closed

482
00:38:54,581 --> 00:38:56,220
and the latching arm latched.

483
00:38:58,100 --> 00:39:01,100
The scrim panels over the windows open or shut

484
00:39:01,101 --> 00:39:03,860
depending on the time of day and the position of the sun.

485
00:39:07,860 --> 00:39:10,680
In addition to shading the building from direct sunlight,

486
00:39:11,120 --> 00:39:14,180
they also help to keep the building cool in another novel way.

487
00:39:14,181 --> 00:39:21,300
The scrim covers the entire east and west sides of the building and stands out 10 inches

488
00:39:21,301 --> 00:39:23,620
from the exterior walls.

489
00:39:23,621 --> 00:39:28,780
In essence, this creates a chimney.

490
00:39:28,781 --> 00:39:33,940
When the sun hits the scrim, it heats up the air in between the scrim and the building.

491
00:39:33,941 --> 00:39:42,880
The hot air rises and pulls cooler air from below, driving what's known as a stack effect.

492
00:39:42,881 --> 00:39:46,840
And that's not the end of the exterior's green design.

493
00:39:46,841 --> 00:39:52,220
The electrical contractor is covering the south side of the building with 43,000 photovoltaic

494
00:39:52,221 --> 00:39:56,440
solar cells that convert sunlight directly into electricity.

495
00:39:56,441 --> 00:40:03,840
What we're looking at here is a photovoltaic system that is going to help cut down electrical

496
00:40:03,841 --> 00:40:06,400
costs to this building.

497
00:40:06,401 --> 00:40:13,900
And it will be producing about 85,000 watts of solar electricity.

498
00:40:13,901 --> 00:40:18,680
Each individual panel creates 28 volts.

499
00:40:18,681 --> 00:40:25,160
This system, if it was in the sun all day, could run, say, 40 homes.

500
00:40:25,161 --> 00:40:29,180
Designers hope the environmentally friendly features of the Caltrans building will help

501
00:40:29,181 --> 00:40:32,800
earn the building a silver lead rating.

502
00:40:32,801 --> 00:40:35,900
that another skyscraper 3,000 miles away

503
00:40:35,901 --> 00:40:38,740
will be pushing green buildings to the max

504
00:40:38,741 --> 00:40:42,180
and into the sky.

505
00:40:42,181 --> 00:40:45,580
The Durst organization is developing the 54-story,

506
00:40:45,581 --> 00:40:48,480
one Bryant Park building in New York City,

507
00:40:48,481 --> 00:40:51,300
which they hope will be the world's first high-rise

508
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:55,700
to reach the coveted platinum lead rating.

509
00:40:55,701 --> 00:40:57,680
And there was absolutely no question

510
00:40:57,681 --> 00:41:01,140
that this building has to have a platinum rating.

511
00:41:01,141 --> 00:41:02,900
That was the goal set by the whole team.

512
00:41:03,780 --> 00:41:08,500
And we want to set an example for others that will change the marketplace.

513
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:16,520
Designed by Cook plus Fox Architects, the building will use significantly less energy

514
00:41:16,521 --> 00:41:20,240
and consume far less water than other buildings of similar size.

515
00:41:22,820 --> 00:41:26,660
Insulated floor-to-ceiling windows will emit as much sunlight as possible.

516
00:41:26,661 --> 00:41:33,280
interior lights will adjust automatically to the amount of daylight coming in

517
00:41:33,281 --> 00:41:39,740
on top of the building there's even going to be a vertical axis wind turbine

518
00:41:39,741 --> 00:41:47,200
the building is actually this faceted crystal and there's a tall mast and a shorter mast

519
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:53,300
the shorter mast is actually intended to be the wind spire the element that generates energy

520
00:41:53,301 --> 00:41:59,960
through the wind. We're putting all of our emphasis on doing sustainable

521
00:41:59,961 --> 00:42:06,500
buildings because this is the right thing to do. Using promising projects such as

522
00:42:06,501 --> 00:42:11,720
One Bryant Park and the Caltrans building as examples, environmental advocates are

523
00:42:11,721 --> 00:42:15,860
hoping green construction will continue to grow.

524
00:42:16,740 --> 00:42:22,580
By the spring of 2004, the outside of the Caltrans structure looks almost

525
00:42:22,581 --> 00:42:23,296
complete.

526
00:42:23,297 --> 00:42:29,580
Although the internal systems aren't finished yet, our building now has a face.

527
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:34,828
A design that's both innovative and environmentally conscious.

528
00:42:34,829 --> 00:42:37,260
Its builders are already prepared

529
00:42:37,261 --> 00:42:40,200
for the reviews that accompany the completion of any megastructure.

530
00:42:40,201 --> 00:42:44,426
There'll be a certain amount of people who look at this building and love it.

531
00:42:44,427 --> 00:42:45,127
Some will

532
00:42:45,128 --> 00:42:48,020
hate it and there'll be some in between that go, uh, well, that's the ones that are hardest

533
00:42:48,021 --> 00:42:51,700
for me. See, that's the one I'd get. Then I'd go, I'm not doing my job.

534
00:42:53,060 --> 00:42:57,740
The Morphosis architects have designed a state-funded building, which is the model of efficiency,

535
00:42:58,260 --> 00:43:01,380
while still holding fast to their personal design convictions.

536
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:08,780
Architects through the ages have always faced tough design decisions.

537
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:17,760
Functional or fun? Elegant or economic? Classic or iconoclastic?

538
00:43:18,020 --> 00:43:22,020
Maybe the best buildings are all of the above.

